      "writtenOn": "2023-09-28T02:08:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "How much runtime overhead if keep using the original phandle design for the color? This change limits the choice of colors. If a project picks a new color, maybe an unusual color, now requires adding the color to the enum. The color is just a link mapping to the duty cycles. It doesn\u0027t have to be mapped to some pre-defined list of colors.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 22,
        "startChar": 23,
        "endLine": 22,
        "endChar": 33
      },
      "revId": "8fa16b19ead920ae187b5285a49c1066173f4f00",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "12daaa9c_fec1437f",
        "filename": "zephyr/dts/bindings/leds/cros-ec,led-policy.yaml",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 22,
      "author": {
        "id": 1548775
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-09-29T01:09:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I purposely made the led-color property not a phandle to keep color definition separate from the LED definition. e.g. if we have 2 LEDs and the color was a phandle, to turn both LEDs blue we would have to set `led-0` to `\u0026led-0-color-blue` and `led-1` to `\u0026led-1-color-blue` instead of both to `LED_BLUE`.\n\nIf we want to support arbitrary colors I can just make the color a string and not a enum. Nothing in the driver itself relies on the fact that color is an enum.\n\nThe only reason color is an enum is to be compatible with `ectool` which has a defined enum of colors. So if we make color user definable any new color will not be supported by ectool.",
      "parentUuid": "261ccb77_7155357b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 22,
        "startChar": 23,
        "endLine": 22,
        "endChar": 33
      },
      "revId": "8fa16b19ead920ae187b5285a49c1066173f4f00",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
