      "lineNbr": 84,
      "author": {
        "id": 1548775
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-09-29T01:09:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For example in the linear transition `cur_color[i].pulse_ns \u003d (next_color[i].pulse_ns - prev_color[i].pulse_ns) * pattern-\u003eticks / duration + prev_color[i].pulse_ns;` the term `(next_color[i].pulse_ns - prev_color[i].pulse_ns)` could be negative. casting the number to a signed int in the middle of the calculation would have undesired behavior when pulse_ns \u003e 2^31.\n\nwhile we could use a lot of if statements to keep the intermediate values strictly positive and change the signs instead, considering the fact that all the LEDs run at less than 1 000 000 it seems better to just use 1 bit in the number to keep track of the direction of transition.\n\nYou might ask why is casting it worse if it\u0027s less than 1 000 000 anyways so there\u0027s no overflow problems. It\u0027s because having it as a signed int allows the compiler to throw errors in the off chance someone does enter a value \u003e 2^31.",
      "parentUuid": "887f99f5_e227a51e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 83,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 84,
        "endChar": 34
      },
